  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Chronic rhinosinusitis without nasal polyps (J32.9)</content>. More than 12 weeks of blockage, discolored drainage, facial pressure and reduced smell, with edema and pus in the middle meatus on endoscopy, confirm chronic rhinosinusitis; no polyps are seen on either side. The past five days are an acute exacerbation with pus. Normal eosinophils and total IgE and no asthma point away from type 2 inflammation. Dr. Lindgren advises a culture-directed antibiotic course for the flare, no oral steroid because there are no polyps, continued irrigation and nasal steroid, and a sinus CT only after about eight more weeks of medical therapy if symptoms persist. [S]<list><item>Amoxicillin-clavulanate 875/125 mg twice daily with food for 10 days; culture result by phone.</item><item>Continue saline irrigation twice daily and fluticasone daily.</item><item>No flying while blocked or in pain; a 48-hour ground trial of the antibiotic before any flight.</item><item>Phone check at the end of the antibiotic course.</item><item>Dental check because the left upper teeth ache.</item><item>SNOT-22 at the preventive visit and at about ten weeks; sinus CT before the ten-week review.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Nasal blockage with suspected chronic rhinosinusitis (R09.81)</content>. Superseded by the diagnosis of chronic rhinosinusitis without nasal polyps today. [S]<list><item>Problem resolved.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Protocol monitoring: items the monitoring protocol calls for at this visit. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Measure</th><th>Interval</th><th>Status</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Survey</td><td>SNOT-22 total score (0 to 110; 20 or more after medical therapy supports surgery)</td><td>At diagnosis, after each treatment step, and after surgery</td><td>Completed today</td><td>58, from 52 at the first visit, during an acute exacerbation</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Facial pain or pressure score (0 to 10)</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>7 of 10, from 6</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Nasal endoscopy (polyps, edema, pus, crusting, healing)</td><td>To confirm the diagnosis, at flares, and after surgery</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Edema and pus in the left middle meatus; no polyps on either side</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Endoscopically guided culture of pus</td><td>When pus is seen and an antibiotic is considered</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Left middle meatus culture sent; result by phone</td></tr><tr><td>Imaging</td><td>CT of the paranasal sinuses (Lund-Mackay score, anatomy, red flags)</td><td>After appropriate medical therapy when surgery is being considered, or at once for complications</td><td>Due</td><td>Not yet: no warning signs, and imaging is planned after about eight more weeks of medical therapy</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Warning signs of complications (eye swelling, vision change, severe headache, stiff neck, confusion)</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>None: no eye swelling or vision change, no severe headache or stiff neck</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Conditions that change management (asthma, cystic fibrosis, immune deficiency, ciliary dyskinesia, aspirin sensitivity, dental source)</td><td>At diagnosis, and if disease does not respond</td><td>Up to date</td><td>Eosinophils and total IgE normal at the first visit; no asthma, aspirin sensitivity, or frequent infections; dental check advised</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Saline irrigation: daily use, technique, and safe water</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Twice daily with distilled water; technique correct</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Intranasal steroid: daily use, technique, nosebleeds</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Daily, aimed away from the septum; no nosebleeds</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Topical decongestant spray (rebound blockage)</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>None since the rebound blockage last winter; advised against</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Oral corticosteroid</td><td>Short course for disease with polyps; not routine without polyps</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>No polyps; an oral steroid is not used for disease without polyps</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Antibiotic for an acute exacerbation</td><td>Only for an acute exacerbation, guided by culture where possible</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Amoxicillin-clavulanate for 10 days started for the flare with pus</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Antifungal treatment</td><td>Not used in chronic rhinosinusitis</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>The guideline advises against topical or systemic antifungals</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Medicines acceptable for pilots and the 48-hour ground trial of a new medicine</td><td>With every new medicine</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Saline and fluticasone acceptable; 48-hour ground trial of the antibiotic; no sedating antihistamine</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Fitness to fly: not flying while blocked or in pain (14 CFR 61.53); sinus and ear clearing on descent</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Not flying while blocked; will fly only when clear</td></tr><tr><td>Referral</td><td>Endoscopic sinus surgery evaluation (appropriateness criteria and shared decision)</td><td>When symptoms persist after appropriate medical therapy with disease on CT</td><td>Due</td><td>To be decided after the CT and the SNOT-22 at about ten weeks</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Goal progress:</paragraph><list><item>SNOT-22 total score below 20 by four months. In progress; target 1923-10-12 [S]</item><item>Fly without sinus or ear pressure pain, and only when she meets the FAA rule of not flying while symptomatic, by four months. In progress; target 1923-10-12 [S]</item><item>Use only medicines acceptable for pilots, with a 48-hour ground trial of each new medicine and no sedating antihistamine. In progress; target 1923-10-12 [S]</item><item>Saline irrigation and the nasal steroid used daily with correct technique. In progress; target 1923-08-17 [S]</item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: Clinical practice guideline (update): adult sinusitis (2015); International consensus statement on allergy and rhinology: rhinosinusitis 2021 (2021); Defining appropriateness criteria for endoscopic sinus surgery during management of uncomplicated adult chronic rhinosinusitis: a RAND/UCLA appropriateness study (2016); Is there a role for antibiotics in the treatment of chronic rhinosinusitis? (review of guideline recommendations, including ICAR 2021) (2022); Guide for Aviation Medical Examiners: Over-the-Counter (OTC) Medications Reference Guide, What OTC Medications Can I Take and Still Be Safe to Fly? (2024); Guide for Aviation Medical Examiners: Allergy - Antihistamine and Immunotherapy Medication (2026)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Culture result by phone and a phone check at the end of the antibiotic course. Preventive visit in two weeks with the SNOT-22. Sinus CT and review at about ten weeks. Next scheduled visit 1923-07-06: periodic adult preventive visit. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
